 ## Runtime semantics
 
 - **Receipts.** `post-commit` writes `.quilt/receipts/<short>.json`:
-  `{commit, changed_cells, key_dials{<alias>{obstruction,freeze}}, ts}`.
+  `{commit, changed_cells, key_dials{<alias>{obstruction,freeze}}, ts, sig}`.
   `ts` is the git commit time (`git log -1 --format=%ct`), never wall clock,
   so receipts reproduce deterministically from history.
+- **Notes receipts.** The hook then attaches the same JSON to the tick
+  commit with `git notes --ref=quilt/receipts` — the receipt rides INSIDE
+  git's object graph, so it travels with the branch on fetch/push where the
+  `.quilt/receipts/` files cannot. `quilt-audit` prints every cell
+  commit's receipt from notes (`git log --notes=quilt/receipts --oneline
+  -- cells/` is the manual equivalent); plain `git clone` does not copy
+  `refs/notes/*`, so `quilt-init` wires the fetch refspec and pulls the
+  notes in.
+- **Signed ticks.** `sig` = fnv1a-64 over
+  `receipt-without-sig || committer-ident || commit-tree` — a cheap
+  transport-level integrity mark (unkeyed, GPG-free) tying the receipt to
+  THAT commit by THAT actor. `quilt-verify` recomputes it for every noted
+  receipt and names any commit whose note no longer matches. It catches
+  transport damage and lazy edits; a tamperer who re-forges the sig passes
+  (use signed commits for real security).
 - **Cascade.** For every `cells/<alias>/links`, dial 14 becomes
   `max(src.dial1 × weight)` over its lines, rounded to 4 decimals (awk).
   Missing sources count as 0. One pass, sorted glob order, no transitive
@@ -87,11 +108,19 @@ Or with the helper: `.quilt/bin/quilt-tick auth 0 0.42`.
    uncommitted when a tick lands.
 5. **`receipts/` and `watch.log` are working-tree artifacts** until someone
